---
/*
  Die Seite, die Ralfs mitten im Gespraech schickt. Sie muss allein stehen
  koennen.

  UMBAU 08.09.2026, Ansage Manuel:

  1. Teil zwei hiess "You never hand us a password." Das war schlicht falsch.
     Korrektur Manuel 08.09.2026, woertlich: die Creatorinnen geben uns das
     Passwort, damit sich unsere Programme verbinden koennen; wir loggen uns
     nie direkt in den Account ein, nur ueber diese Programme. Der ganze
     Abschnitt ist danach neu geschrieben.
  2. Keine Toolnamen. Die zwei Werkzeuge standen frueher hier namentlich.
     Jetzt: "the programs we built for this".
  3. Der Name der Fan-Plattform steht nirgends mehr.
  4. Jeder Teil hat ein Bild.

  Der Satz zur Einkommenszusage unten steht wortgleich in
  scripts/allowed-phrases.txt. Wer ihn umformuliert, muss die Freigabezeile
  dort mitziehen, sonst bricht der Copy-Check.
*/
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CtaBlock from '../components/CtaBlock.astro';
import Split from '../components/Split.astro';
import Photo from '../components/Photo.astro';
---

<Base
  title="How it works | Wunder"
  description="Three parts you can check: your own portal, the programs that do the work, and a check on everyone who writes to your fans."
>
  <Split
    image="hero-creator-talk.webp"
    alt="A creator and Lauris sit across from each other at a podcast table and talk."
    width={640}
    height={360}
    caption="We explain all of this again on the call, in your own words."
    eager
  >
    <h1>How the work is done.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Three parts. You can check each one. You do not have to take our word for it.
    </p>
    <CtaBlock secondary />
  </Split>

  <Split
    id="portal"
    image="portal-login-desktop.webp"
    alt="The portal login screen on a laptop, with fields for email and password."
    width={1200}
    height={750}
    caption="This is the door, not the rooms behind it. No creator numbers are shown here."
    flip
  >
    <p class="eyebrow">Part one</p>
    <h2>You see the same screen we do.</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      You can look at your account at any time. You do not have to ask us. You see the same number
      we see.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every post goes through our own portal. We read each number where it comes from. We do not
      guess it. We built the whole chain ourselves. So there is nothing we could tell you that we
      cannot also show you.
    </p>
  </Split>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <ol>
        <li>
          First we read your last thirty days. We look at what your account did on its own. That
          number is your starting point.
        </li>
        <li>
          Each night our system works out your middle day of the last thirty days. Then it scores
          every post against your own middle day, not against some agency average.
        </li>
        <li>A post is scored three days after it goes up. Before that it is too young to judge.</li>
        <li>
          You log into your own portal. You see views, reach and followers there. You can look at
          seven, thirty and ninety days. You see the daily line and the period before it. You see
          the content plan.
        </li>
        <li>
          Two things are missing there on purpose. You cannot see who a single fan is. You cannot
          see how our team is doing inside. That limit stops your own login from opening other
          people's data.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <CtaBlock />
    </div>
  </section>

  <Split
    id="access"
    image="portal-login-mobile.webp"
    alt="The same login screen on a phone."
    width={430}
    height={932}
    caption="Your portal login is your own. It is kept apart from our staff system."
    narrow
  >
    <p class="eyebrow">Part two</p>
    <h2>What happens with your password.</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      You give us the password for your page. Our programs need it so they can connect. That is the
      only reason we ask for it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nobody here opens your account by hand. Every post, every message and every number runs
      through the programs we built for this. So there is a record of what was done, and when.
    </p>
  </Split>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <ol>
        <li>
          Your posts go out through our own programs. A person picks the post and the time. The
          program does the rest.
        </li>
        <li>
          The chat with your fans and your paid files run through the same kind of program. Again,
          no person signs in as you.
        </li>
        <li>Our team signs in to our own systems with company accounts. No shared logins.</li>
        <li>
          Your portal login is its own thing. A creator login can never hold a staff role. We read
          who you are from a signed cookie only. So nobody can change a number in the link and land
          in someone else's data.
        </li>
        <li>
          We can switch your portal access off at once. It stops on the next click, not on the next
          login. Taking you out of the portal deletes nothing. It only sets a date. So a wrong
          click is one reset, not a loss.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Split
    id="chatting"
    image="lauris-speaking.webp"
    alt="Lauris sits at a microphone and explains something. He is looking at the person across the table."
    width={640}
    height={360}
    caption="The chat desk has its own managers. They read the work back."
    flip
  >
    <p class="eyebrow">Part three</p>
    <h2>We check the people who write to your fans.</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      You already make the work. What is missing is the selling. That is the part we do, with
      people we check.
    </p>
    <p>
      Someone writes to your fans in your name. That work gets read back, and not by the person who
      wrote it. Chat is the only thing we do in your name. So we built a check for it. It runs to a
      plan, and nobody on the chat team can switch it off.
    </p>
  </Split>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <ol>
        <li>
          Twice a week our system reads the shift plan. It pulls the chat log of the last finished
          shift. It scores that log against a written score sheet. Every version of the sheet is
          kept.
        </li>
        <li>
          Only human messages get a score. Each message is sorted by source first. Messages sent by
          the system count as background, not as work.
        </li>
        <li>
          There is a fixed pass mark. Several people score, and we take the middle score. Some
          mistakes fail on their own. We also measure how fast the reply came.
        </li>
        <li>The chat logs themselves are never saved in the database for this report.</li>
        <li>
          Messages sent by the system pass one more test. It is built into the code. It blocks any
          message that hints at an age under eighteen. It blocks any request for money outside the
          platform. The point is simple. An automatic message must not put your account at risk.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Chatters are people. Now and then a message comes out wrong. We do not hide that. We tell
        you, and we put it right with you. Most creators are fine with it once they know how we
        handle it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Split
    image="creator-tells-her-story.webp"
    alt="A creator talks and uses her hands to explain how her account grew."
    width={640}
    height={360}
    caption="You keep the last word on your own account."
  >
    <h2>What we do not do.</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No fee up front. No fee before you have earned.</li>
      <li>No access to your money. Your payout reaches your bank first.</li>
      <li>No post goes out without your yes.</li>
      <li>No old post stays in the plan once you ask us to stop it.</li>
      <li>We do not guarantee your income. Nobody honest can.</li>
    </ul>
    <CtaBlock />
  </Split>
</Base>
